    {
      "message_id": 5,
      "session_id": "rec-s02-20261106T1219Z",
      "user_id": "rec-u02",
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Could two processes write the same sequence number to it?",
      "status": null,
      "query_id": null,
      "tokens": 15,
      "created_at": 1793967621.5490763,
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "message_id": 6,
      "session_id": "rec-s02-20261106T1219Z",
      "user_id": "rec-u02",
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Evidence: `prash/audit.py:L67-L80`. - [inferred] Because the sequence is read and then appended without a lock, two processes appending at the same moment can write the same `seq`; the log stays append-only but the sequence is not guaranteed unique under concurrency. - [implemented] The circuit-breaker state is written with a plain `write_text` (no temporary file and rename). [1]",
      "status": "GROUNDED",
